Instruction: Read the following segment of an inter-company merger protocol and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is classified as superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Protocol: Section 9.7. Offer Evaluations and Recommendations. 
(a) In the event that a New Acquisition Proposal is received, the Board must issue a preliminary determination of its status as a Superior Proposal only after (i) confirming that, in addition to the financial metrics set forth in Schedule M, the proposal complies with the strategic alignment criteria detailed in Appendix O, and (ii) verifying that consultation with both the Company's independent legal advisors and a financial analyst of internationally acclaimed repute has been completed; 
(b) Should negotiations with the proposing party result in modifications to any material term, the Board is required to reassess the proposal's status to ensure simultaneous compliance with fiduciary obligations and non-financial strategic goals; 
(c) A formal Written Notification must be dispatched to the Parent Company at least five Business Days before reinitiating any offer review process, during which the Board is further mandated to consider any emergent regulatory or operational changes; 
(d) The assessment process explicitly requires that all evaluations incorporate both quantitative (financial) and qualitative (strategic, legal, and operational) considerations to uphold the fiduciary duties owed to the Company’s shareholders.
