Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: Section 4.9 – Determination of Offer Superiority. (a) For an Acquisition Proposal to be deemed a Superior Proposal, the Board is required to: 
   (i) Obtain an independent financial analysis from a nationally recognized advisor;
   (ii) Conduct a comprehensive operational synergy evaluation;
   (iii) Consult with external legal counsel to assess compliance with applicable regulatory, environmental, and fiduciary standards;
   (iv) Undertake a rigorous risk management and strategic assessment.
(b) Notwithstanding clause (a)(i), if an Acquisition Proposal presents unusually favorable financial terms compared to competing offers, the Board may choose to abbreviate the review process; however, such abbreviation does not exempt the Board from the mandatory evaluations specified in clauses (a)(ii) through (a)(iv).
(c) Any amendment to the financial terms, or to any risk management or operational strategy, shall necessitate a complete re-evaluation under the standards of Section 4.9 and Section 7.2. [End of Document]
